column60:
I decided to try to figure it out from this angle.  What we have is a random walk where, with probability 0.51 we go forward, and with probability 0.49 we go backward.  If we ever reach 10, we quit.  I wrote a program to compute the cumulative probability that we stop at 10 over time.<p><pre><code>  #include <stdio.h>
  #include <math.h>

  #define GOAL 10
  #define MAXN 20000

  double p = 0.51;
  double pr[2][MAXN + 1 + GOAL];

  #define PR_(t, x) (pr[(t) % 2][MAXN + (x)])

  int main()
  {
    int n, i;
    double totalp = 0;

    PR_(0, 0) = 1;
    for (n = 1; n <= MAXN; n++) {
        PR_(n, -n) = PR_(n-1, -n+1) * (1 - p);
        for (i = -n+1; i < GOAL; i++)
            PR_(n, i) = PR_(n-1, i-1) * p + PR_(n-1, i+1) * (1 - p);
        totalp += PR_(n-1, GOAL-1) * p;
        printf("n=%d p=%g
", n, totalp);
        if (totalp >= 0.99)
            break;
    }

    return 0;
  }
</code></pre>
The results:<p><pre><code>  n=5488 p=0.989997
  n=5489 p=0.989997
  n=5490 p=0.990005
</code></pre>
You get to quit much sooner if you can walk away as soon as you have $10!
